=== Swabia and Bohemia ===
[[File:15-02-05-Schwäbisch-Gmünd-RalfR-DSCF2140-30.jpg|thumb|left|Parish Church of the Holy Cross in Schwäbisch-Gmünd (1320)]]
In [[Swabia]] and [[Bohemia]], the spread of the Gothic style is linked to the work of the Parlers, a dynasty of master builders who built a vast network of professional relationships. Originally, the term 'parler' indicated the first assistant to the master builder (''magister operis''), and only from the 14th century onwards did it become the family's surname. The patriarch was Heinrich I the Elder, whose descendants extended their artistic influence across a large part of Bohemia, in the German-Austrian area, reaching as far as [[Brabant]] and [[Flanders]].<ref name="Prina2009">{{cite book | author=Francesca Prina | title=Storia dell'architettura gotica | publisher=Electa | year=2009 | location=Milano | p=109 }}</ref> In their works, recurring elements are recognized in the layout of spaces and the conformation of complex vaults, which justifies the term ''Parlergotik''.<ref name="Grodecki1978">{{cite book | author=Louis Grodecki | title=Architettura gotica | publisher=Electa | year=1978 | location=Milano | others=with contributions by Anne Prache and Roland Recht, Italian translation by Anna Bacigalupo | p=142}}</ref>

Heinrich Parler and his son Peter both worked at the parish church of the Holy Cross in Schwäbisch-Gmünd (1320), in Swabia, reviving the model of the hall church based on the simplicity of the mendicant orders, but with innovative solutions in the choir (begun in 1350).

[[File:Prag 2013 063.jpg|thumb|St. Vitus Cathedral in Prague, vault ceiling of the choir (1356)]]
[[Peter Parler]], in particular, was one of the great protagonists of the Late Gothic. In 1356, he was summoned to [[Prague]] by Emperor [[Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor|Charles IV]] to complete [[St. Vitus Cathedral]], begun in 1344 by the French architect Mathieu d'Arras. The sovereign had transformed Prague into the capital of the Empire and wanted to expand it with an ambitious monumental plan, which included a Gothic palace, [[Karlstejn Castle]], and buildings for the new [[University of Prague]]. 

Peter Parler demonstrated great inventiveness in St. Vitus: he broke the fluid rhythm of the ambulatory, added the monumental square-plan chapel dedicated to [[Saint Wenceslas]], and crowned the interiors with a pioneering fillet vault (or intersecting ribs in a mesh pattern, which evokes the solutions of English Gothic), where the ribs intertwine in a continuous fashion. To Peter Parler we also owe the All Saints' Chapel on the Hradčany hill (using the Sainte-Chapelle as a model), the famous Charles Bridge over the Vltava (1357), the choir of [[Kolín]] (1360), and the [[Frauenkirche]] in Nuremberg.<ref name="Grodecki1978-pp144-145">{{cite book | author=Louis Grodecki | title=Architettura gotica | publisher=Electa | year=1978 | location=Milano | others=with contributions by Anne Prache and Roland Recht, Italian translation by Anna Bacigalupo | pp=144-145 }}</ref>

Family members were active throughout Europe: Wenzel was architect of the cathedral of Vienna (1400–1404), Johann IV worked on the choir of [[Kutná Hora]], and Heinrich III was at the construction site of [[Milan Cathedral]] in 1392. Many builders drew inspiration from them, such as Ulrich von Ensingen (who designed the enormous tower of [[Ulm Minster]], begun in 1377, and worked on the facade of Strasbourg). At the end of the 14th century, the Parler style was also adapted to the brick churches of the north, as in [[Stargard]] and [[Brandenburg]].<ref name="Watkin2007-p173">{{cite book | author=David Watkin | title=Storia dell'architettura occidentale | publisher=Zanichelli | location=Bologna | year=2007 | pp=173-175}}</ref>

